---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI EAS builds" activeNav="/cli/builds/eas-builds">
  <h1>EAS builds</h1>
  <p>
    An EAS build is one that Expo runs. The build record on this side mirrors it: you register
    the EAS build id, and the platform tracks the Expo build against the app's history so it
    appears next to every other build instead of living only in Expo's dashboard.
  </p>

  <h2>Registering an EAS build</h2>
  <p>
    Create the record with <code>--reporting-mode eas</code> and the Expo build id.
    <code>--expo-build-id</code> is required in this mode — without it the API has nothing to
    mirror:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`EXPO_BUILD_ID=$(eas build --platform ios --profile production --json --non-interactive | jq -er '.[0].id')

appmctl builds create \\
  --profile appstore \\
  --reporting-mode eas \\
  --expo-build-id "$EXPO_BUILD_ID" \\
  --tag ios:#3b82f6 --tag eas:#7c3aed`} />
  <p>
    The record starts as <code>pending</code> and follows the Expo build from there. Check what
    the request will look like before wiring it into a pipeline:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --dry-run builds create --profile appstore \\
  --reporting-mode eas --expo-build-id 22222222-3333-4444-5555-666666666666

# dry-run: no request sent
# method: POST
# url: https://manager.example.com/api/v1/acme-mobile/apps/customer-app/builds
# body: {
#   "request_uuid": "...",
#   "profile_type": "appstore",
#   "reporting_mode": "eas",
#   "expo_build_id": "22222222-3333-4444-5555-666666666666"
# }`} />

  <h2>Handing a manual build over to EAS</h2>
  <p>
    A build may begin in manual mode while a runner prepares or submits the Expo build. Once
    Expo returns its build id, patch that id onto the existing record:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`APP_MANAGER_BUILD_ID=$(
  appmctl --json builds create --profile appstore | jq -er '.data.id'
)
export APP_MANAGER_BUILD_ID

appmctl logs post --build-id "$APP_MANAGER_BUILD_ID" --content "Submitting to EAS…\n"
EXPO_BUILD_ID=$(eas build --platform ios --profile production --json --non-interactive | jq -er '.[0].id')
appmctl builds patch "$APP_MANAGER_BUILD_ID" --expo-build-id "$EXPO_BUILD_ID"`} />
  <p>
    The patch validates the id against the linked Expo project, switches the same record to
    <code>eas</code> reporting, and starts polling. Existing manual logs remain at the start
    of the combined log; each poll replaces only the Expo portion, so output is not duplicated.
    Manual log writes return <code>409</code> after the handoff because Expo is then the source
    of truth.
  </p>

  <h2>What the CLI will not do here</h2>
  <p>
    Like provider-driven builds, an EAS record is a mirror. Status and log writes are refused
    with <code>409</code>, because Expo is the source of truth for both:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Command</th><th>Result</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>builds patch --status ...</code></td><td><code>409</code> — Expo owns the status</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>stream</code>, <code>logs post</code></td><td><code>409</code> — manual reporting mode only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>builds patch --tag ...</code></td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>artifacts upload</code></td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>builds wait</code></td><td>Allowed, and the useful one</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Waiting on an Expo build</h2>
  <p>
    <code>wait</code> works the same as anywhere else, which lets one CI job gate on an Expo
    build without talking to Expo directly:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`BUILD_ID=$(
  appmctl --json builds create --profile appstore \\
    --reporting-mode eas --expo-build-id "$EXPO_BUILD_ID" |
    jq -er '.data.id'
)

appmctl builds wait "$BUILD_ID" --timeout 5400   # EAS queues can be long`} />
  <p>
    How quickly the mirrored status moves depends on how often the platform syncs with Expo.
    Automatic sync has to be enabled on the linked project for the record to advance on its own
    — see <a href="/cli/integrations/eas">EAS</a> for <code>eas auto-sync</code> and
    <code>eas sync</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the record</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --json builds get "$BUILD_ID" |
  jq '.data | {status, reporting_mode, expo_build_id, started_at, finished_at}'`} />
  <p>
    <code>expo_build_id</code> is present only for records in this mode, which is the quickest
    way to tell an EAS build apart from a manual one in a script.
  </p>

  <h2>Attaching artifacts</h2>
  <p>
    Expo keeps its own artifacts. Uploading them here is worth it when you want one place to
    look regardless of who built what:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`ARTIFACT_URL=$(eas build:view "$EXPO_BUILD_ID" --json | jq -er '.artifacts.buildUrl')
curl -fsSL "$ARTIFACT_URL" -o app.ipa

appmctl artifacts upload --build-id "$BUILD_ID" \\
  --name app.ipa --metadata '{"source":"eas"}' --file app.ipa`} />

  <h2>Identifiers and credentials</h2>
  <p>
    EAS builds sign with credentials Expo holds. To keep those in step with this platform, use
    <code>eas sync-identifiers</code> to push identifiers up, and <code>eas provision</code> or
    <code>eas attach-credential</code> to hand signing material to Expo. All of that is on the
    <a href="/cli/integrations/eas">EAS</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>builds:write</code> to register the record, <code>builds:read</code> to wait on it,
    <code>artifacts:write</code> to attach files. Registering an EAS build does not require any
    integration scope — that is only needed for the <code>eas</code> command itself.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
